# Build context is the REPOSITORY ROOT (see apps/backend/Dockerfile), because a
# --frozen-lockfile install in a Bun workspace needs the root `bun.lock` and
# every workspace manifest. A wider context means a wider blast radius, so the
# exclusions below matter more than they would for a single-package build.

# --- Secrets. The entire point of this file. ---------------------------------
# Globbed at every depth, and deliberately covering `.env.test` and friends
# rather than just `.env`: anything matching this shape is a credential until
# proven otherwise. `.env.example` is excluded too — it holds placeholders, but
# the image has no use for it, and an allowlist of "safe" env files is exactly
# the kind of thing that rots.
#
# The Dockerfile also copies named paths instead of `COPY . .`, so a secret
# would have to defeat both this file and an explicit COPY to reach a layer.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key

# --- Never useful in an image ------------------------------------------------
**/node_modules
.git
.gitignore
**/.turbo
.claude

# --- Host build output, which the image rebuilds or does not need ------------
**/dist
**/build
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.eslintcache

# --- Not part of the running service -----------------------------------------
# The backend runs TypeScript directly, so only `src` is needed at runtime;
# tests, docs and the plan are context the container should not carry.
specs
**/tests
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
**/README.md
AGENTS.md
DEPLOYMENT.md
**/eslint.config.js

# The web app and the shared lint config contribute nothing to the API image
# except their manifests — which a --frozen-lockfile install in a workspace
# cannot do without (see the Dockerfile header). Excluding the contents and
# re-admitting the one needed file keeps the context honest about why each
# path is there.
apps/web/**
!apps/web/package.json
packages/eslint-config/**
!packages/eslint-config/package.json
